# Root crontab for the v2 box. Installed on the host, not in a container.
#
#   Install:   crontab /opt/wtg-weather/infra/cron/crontab
#   Verify:    crontab -l
#   Backup first, if anything is already scheduled:
#              crontab -l > /root/crontab.$(date -u +%FT%TZ).bak
#
# `crontab <file>` REPLACES the whole table — it does not merge. This file is
# therefore the complete set of scheduled jobs, and anything installed by hand
# and not written down here is lost on the next install. That is the intended
# property: `crontab -l` and this file must agree (DEVELOPMENT_PLAN.md § WS-E
# acceptance).
#
# cron gives a job a near-empty PATH, not a login shell's. Every script here
# needs `docker` (/usr/bin), and the pipeline ones additionally need `uv`, which
# the installer puts in root's ~/.local/bin — the exact trap that makes
# `ssh host 'uv ...'` fail while `bash -lc 'uv ...'` works. tippecanoe and
# pmtiles are in /usr/local/bin. All three are spelled out rather than left to
# whatever the shell inherits.
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:/root/.local/bin

# No MTA is configured on this box, so cron's default "mail the output to root"
# produces an undeliverable-mail error per run and nothing a human ever reads.
# The log files below are the record. That does mean a failed job is silent
# until someone looks — pointing an alert at these logs (or at GlitchTip, per
# WS-E step 5) is the follow-up, not something this file can do.
MAILTO=""

# Every job appends to its own log and merges stderr into it, because each
# script's progress goes to stdout and its failures to stderr, and reading a
# failed run means reading both interleaved. Rotation is logrotate's business;
# these files are small except in the January week.

# Nightly 02:00 UTC: encrypted Postgres dump to Backblaze B2.
0 2 * * * /opt/wtg-weather/infra/scripts/backup-postgres.sh >> /var/log/wtg-backup.log 2>&1

# Weekly Sun 03:00 UTC: scrape the six governments, consolidate, and rebuild
# the tiles + purge the CDN only if a country's advisory level actually moved.
# Runs `uv` on the host — the pipeline is not containerised.
# NOTE: travel.state.gov 403s from this box, so the US dump is refreshed by
# hand from a permitted network; `wtg process advisories` logs ADVISORY_STALE
# when that has not happened recently. See infra/CLAUDE.md § "US advisory
# scrape (Cloudflare 403)".
0 3 * * 0 /opt/wtg-weather/infra/scripts/weekly-advisories.sh >> /var/log/wtg-advisories.log 2>&1

# Weekly Mon 04:00 UTC: recompute alert matches, email on transition.
# Runs the job inside the `api` container (it needs Postgres, which is on the
# internal-only network); the script holds its own lock and is safe to retry.
# Monday rather than Sunday so it lands after the advisory run, which is the
# only thing that republishes data mid-year.
0 4 * * 1 /opt/wtg-weather/infra/scripts/weekly-alerts.sh >> /var/log/wtg-alerts.log 2>&1

# Yearly Jan 15 04:00 UTC: full ERA5 rebuild — download the new year,
# re-aggregate, percentiles, both tile tiers + CDN purge, republish the SSR
# bundle, and recreate `web` so the ISR cache lets the new numbers through.
# Mid-January because Copernicus lags the calendar by two to four weeks.
#
# This one runs for DAYS at admin-2 scale and needs room on the data volume;
# it is the one entry here that wants a human watching it start. Print the
# plan first with:  DRY_RUN=1 /opt/wtg-weather/infra/scripts/yearly-era5.sh
0 4 15 1 * /opt/wtg-weather/infra/scripts/yearly-era5.sh >> /var/log/wtg-era5.log 2>&1
